      {
        "id": "p46-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Use long division to show that $P(x)=2x^4+3x^3-12x^2-7x+6$ is divisible by $x^2-x-2$ and express $P(x)$ as a product of four linear factors.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(x)=(x-2)(x+1)(2x-1)(x+3)$. Long division gives quotient $2x^2+5x-3$ and remainder $0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{array}{r|l}&2x^2+5x-3\\\\\\hline x^2-x-2&2x^4+3x^3-12x^2-7x+6\\\\&\\underline{-(2x^4-2x^3-4x^2)}\\\\&5x^3-8x^2-7x+6\\\\&\\underline{-(5x^3-5x^2-10x)}\\\\&-3x^2+3x+6\\\\&\\underline{-(-3x^2+3x+6)}\\\\&0\\end{array}$$\nThe remainder is zero, so $P(x)$ is divisible by $x^2-x-2$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}P(x)&=(x^2-x-2)(2x^2+5x-3)\\\\&=(x-2)(x+1)(2x-1)(x+3).\\end{aligned}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      }
    ]
  },
